The foolish crow follows the desire of sensual pleasures. It is something that cannot be known, heard, seen, understood, realized, or perceived through wisdom. He says, "The Buddha is of no use to me," and thus makes it known. He says, "Now is not the time to reveal it."

Among those particles such as na and no, the particle 'na' also exists in the sense of comparison (upamāna). In such instances as "The commander Sumukha, who has wings with the color of gold, acts as a blind man acts in the dark," by taking the particle 'na' and associating it with the word 'kataṃ', the term 'na kataṃ' gains the meaning "as if done."

The particle 'no' also exists in the sense of a question (pucchā). In the passage, "Great King, do you recall (abhijānāsi no) having asked this question to other recluses and brahmins?", 'abhijānāsi no' means 'abhijānāsi nu' (do you know/recall?). The particle 'no' also exists in the sense of emphasis (avadhāraṇa). It carries the meaning, "There is indeed no treasure equal to the Tathāgata." However, by way of extracting the meaning, the word 'no' also functions in the nominative, accusative, dative, and genitive cases. In those instances, it is not a particle (nipāta) but a pronoun (sabbanāma).

The word 'mā', when it functions as a noun, refers to either the moon or to glory (siri).

The particle 'a' is also seen in the senses of increase (vuddhi), that state (tabbhāva), and so on. Thus it has been said:
"The letter 'a' exists in the sense of negation, increase, and that state;
in the sense of 'other' and in the sense of similarity;
in the sense of opposition, blame, voidness;
and in the sense of absence."

Among those meanings, the sense of negation has already been mentioned. In "asekkhā dhammā" etc., it exists in the sense of increase. In "anavajjamariṭṭhaṃ" etc., it exists in the sense of that state (tabbhāva).
